From 63c562dec63fd1505932d1e7f42bca62d6463002 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: StonkGuy <68372320+StonkGuy@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 21:40:02 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 23/25] LinuxEmulation: deliver guest reads of the call/ret
 shadow-stack guard pages instead of spinning

The SIGSEGV handler took every fault inside a thread's call/ret shadow-stack allocation (the stack and its two guard pages)
for a shadow-stack overflow or underflow: it reset REG_CALLRET_SP (x25) to the default location and resumed the faulting
instruction. That only fixes a fault taken by a push or pop through x25. The guard pages are also listed in the guest's
/proc/self/maps ("[anon:FEXMem_CallRetStacks]", ---p), and code that reads its maps and probes every listed range under a
fault handler touches them with an ordinary load. Resetting x25 does not change that load, so it faulted again forever: one
thread at 100% CPU, mostly kernel time, nothing ever delivered to the guest. An anti-tamper bootstrapper does exactly this
scan (it reads /proc/self/maps and probes each range) and never got past it.

Treat the fault as a shadow-stack overflow/underflow only when its address is where an x25 access lands (stp [x25, #-16]!
writes x25-16..x25-1, ldp [x25] reads x25..x25+15); anything else falls through to the normal paths and is delivered to
the guest as SIGSEGV, as a read of an inaccessible page is on Linux. Cost: two instructions on a SIGSEGV that hits the
allocation; nothing on any other path.

tests/maps-probe reads /proc/self/maps and probes every ---p mapping: native x86-64 PASS; the 0001-0020 build hangs on the first
FEXMem_CallRetStacks guard page; with this change PASS.

Upstream FEX main has the identical check (SyscallsSMCTracking.cpp, HandleSegfault); the Windows frontends'
CallRetStack::HandleAccessViolation have the same shape and are not touched here.

Base: FEX-2610 14c92681 + patches 0001-0022.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 .../LinuxSyscalls/SyscallsSMCTracking.cpp           | 13 ++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/Source/Tools/LinuxEmulation/LinuxSyscalls/SyscallsSMCTracking.cpp b/Source/Tools/LinuxEmulation/LinuxSyscalls/SyscallsSMCTracking.cpp
index 342223c1f..ffbb492cc 100644
--- a/Source/Tools/LinuxEmulation/LinuxSyscalls/SyscallsSMCTracking.cpp
+++ b/Source/Tools/LinuxEmulation/LinuxSyscalls/SyscallsSMCTracking.cpp
@@ -52,9 +52,16 @@ bool SyscallHandler::HandleSegfault(FEXCore::Core::InternalThreadState* Thread,
   auto ThreadObject = FEX::HLE::ThreadManager::GetStateObjectFromFEXCoreThread(Thread);
   auto CallRetStackInfo = ThreadObject->GetCallRetStackInfo();
   if (FaultAddress >= CallRetStackInfo.AllocationBase && FaultAddress < CallRetStackInfo.AllocationEnd) {
-    // Reset REG_CALLRET_SP to the default location to allow for underflows/overflows
-    ArchHelpers::Context::SetArmReg(ucontext, 25, CallRetStackInfo.DefaultLocation);
-    return true;
+    // Only a push or pop through REG_CALLRET_SP (stp [x25, #-16]! / ldp [x25]) is a shadow-stack overflow or underflow. The
+    // guest can also read the guard pages itself: they are listed in its /proc/self/maps, and code that probes every mapped
+    // range (anti-tamper scanners do) touches them. Resetting x25 does not fix such an access, so it would fault again forever;
+    // it is an ordinary guest fault and must be delivered as one.
+    const uint64_t CallRetSP = ArchHelpers::Context::GetArmReg(ucontext, 25);
+    if (FaultAddress - (CallRetSP - 16) < 32) {
+      // Reset REG_CALLRET_SP to the default location to allow for underflows/overflows
+      ArchHelpers::Context::SetArmReg(ucontext, 25, CallRetStackInfo.DefaultLocation);
+      return true;
+    }
   }
 
